1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:11,376
From one of the most ancient achievements of flood control in history, to the largest dam in the world, and the feats of construction and engineering that made them

2
00:00:11,377 --> 00:00:12,077
possible.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Now, China's Three Gorges Dam on Modern Mars.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
It will tower 607 feet in height, and its length will span nearly a mile and a half.

5
00:00:48,880 --> 00:00:55,040
Its volume, when completed, will equal 44 great pyramids, making it one of the largest

6
00:00:55,041 --> 00:00:58,220
structures ever built.

7
00:00:58,221 --> 00:01:06,460
At peak output, its 26 gigantic turbines, each weighing 400 tons, will generate 18,200

8
00:01:06,461 --> 00:01:13,360
megawatts of electricity, equivalent to the output of 18 nuclear power plants.

9
00:01:13,361 --> 00:01:17,800
It will be the only other man-made object besides the Great Wall of China that is visible

10
00:01:17,801 --> 00:01:19,600
from the moon.

11
00:01:19,601 --> 00:01:22,700
This is China's enormous Three Gorges Dam.

12
00:01:28,220 --> 00:01:42,220
The Three Gorges Dam Project in the NC River is the largest ongoing civil engineering project

13
00:01:43,180 --> 00:01:45,380
in the world today.

14
00:01:45,381 --> 00:01:51,280
And some people have characterized it as going to be the eighth wonder of the world.

15
00:01:51,281 --> 00:01:55,940
The cost of Three Gorges is about $20 to $30 billion.

16
00:01:55,941 --> 00:02:01,080
The total concrete placement is about 27 million cubic meters.

17
00:02:01,081 --> 00:02:06,580
That's why the Three Gorges is called the largest dam.

18
00:02:06,581 --> 00:02:09,320
Just as massive structures such as Hoover Dam

19
00:02:09,321 --> 00:02:11,060
helped advance economic development

20
00:02:11,061 --> 00:02:14,760
in the Western United States, supporters see Three Gorges

21
00:02:14,761 --> 00:02:17,820
as the concrete key to a new China,

22
00:02:17,821 --> 00:02:20,940
controlling devastating floods while bringing the benefits

23
00:02:20,941 --> 00:02:25,300
of clean hydroelectric power and extensive river navigation

24
00:02:25,301 --> 00:02:29,440
to one of the most underdeveloped areas of their nation.

25
00:02:29,441 --> 00:02:31,460
Legions of critics, however, see the project

26
00:02:31,461 --> 00:02:35,320
in a more ominous light, citing a litany of concerns ranging

27
00:02:35,321 --> 00:02:38,060
from potential environmental damage

28
00:02:38,061 --> 00:02:41,440
to the permanent inundation of scores of towns and cities

29
00:02:41,441 --> 00:02:44,680
by the rising waters of the Three Gorges Reservoir

30
00:02:44,681 --> 00:02:47,440
and the flooding of the spectacular and world-renowned

31
00:02:47,441 --> 00:02:51,880
Three Gorges, for which the dam is named.

32
00:02:51,881 --> 00:02:53,780
Although the Three Gorges Dam represents

33
00:02:53,781 --> 00:02:56,480
the pinnacle of modern engineering.

34
00:02:56,481 --> 00:02:59,480
Its origins are deeply rooted in the past,

35
00:02:59,481 --> 00:03:02,680
flowing from the same tradition of monumental public projects

36
00:03:02,681 --> 00:03:05,800
that inspired the Great Wall itself.

37
00:03:05,801 --> 00:03:07,960
It's a tradition that has been the hallmark

38
00:03:07,961 --> 00:03:11,240
of Chinese civilization for millennia.

39
00:03:11,241 --> 00:03:14,300
Nowhere is this more in evidence than along the great river

40
00:03:14,301 --> 00:03:17,780
that is both the blessing and the bane of southern China,

41
00:03:17,781 --> 00:03:19,700
the waterway that the Three Gorges Dam

42
00:03:19,701 --> 00:03:23,780
is supposed to finally tame, the mighty Yangtze,

43
00:03:23,781 --> 00:03:35,060
It is the lifeline of southern China, providing transportation, food, and water for irrigation

44
00:03:35,061 --> 00:03:39,320
to an area that is home to over 380 million people.

45
00:03:39,321 --> 00:03:47,140
Its watershed encompasses 700,000 square miles, a fifth of China's total land area.

46
00:03:47,141 --> 00:03:52,220
It irrigates more than a third of China's agricultural output and carries three quarters

47
00:03:52,221 --> 00:03:56,220
of China's internal waterborne commerce.

48
00:03:56,221 --> 00:03:59,760
This is the Yangtze River, one of the longest waterways

49
00:03:59,761 --> 00:04:02,160
on the globe.

50
00:04:02,161 --> 00:04:04,260
Fed by the waters of the many rivers and streams

51
00:04:04,261 --> 00:04:06,460
flowing from the vast mountainous regions

52
00:04:06,461 --> 00:04:09,440
of the Chinese interior, the Yangtze Basin

53
00:04:09,441 --> 00:04:11,540
has been populated for thousands of years

54
00:04:11,541 --> 00:04:16,040
by farmers and hunters, such as the trackers of the Shenongxi,

55
00:04:16,041 --> 00:04:19,420
who even today must pull their shallow craft miles upstream

56
00:04:19,421 --> 00:04:24,180
against relentless currents to return to their homes.

57
00:04:24,181 --> 00:04:27,160
The Yancy River is very difficult to navigate

58
00:04:27,161 --> 00:04:29,020
in the natural state.

59
00:04:29,021 --> 00:04:32,020
There is a full of rapids and very dangerous.

60
00:04:32,021 --> 00:04:34,480
A lot of people die from that.

61
00:04:34,481 --> 00:04:36,160
And also during the low flow period,

62
00:04:36,161 --> 00:04:39,020
you have to have manual, you know,

63
00:04:39,021 --> 00:04:41,380
tow the boat upstream.

64
00:04:41,381 --> 00:04:44,060
One of the purposes of Sri Gurdjieff's Den

65
00:04:44,061 --> 00:04:46,860
will have enough water depth so the navigation

66
00:04:46,861 --> 00:04:51,860
and be much more efficient and safe and low cost.

67
00:04:52,860 --> 00:04:56,860
Better navigation is only one of the traditional needs for dams.

68
00:04:56,861 --> 00:05:00,860
Dams are constructed for many different reasons,

69
00:05:00,861 --> 00:05:05,860
including flood control, power production, irrigation, water storage,

70
00:05:05,861 --> 00:05:08,860
and something as simple as recreation.

71
00:05:08,861 --> 00:05:12,860
Dams have been constructed for thousands and thousands of years.

72
00:05:12,861 --> 00:05:15,660
the oldest known dam was constructed on the Nile

73
00:05:15,760 --> 00:05:18,360
over 4,000 years ago.

74
00:05:18,460 --> 00:05:20,900
Just as the Nile is for Egypt,

75
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:24,000
the Yangtze Valley is the cradle of Chinese civilization

76
00:05:24,100 --> 00:05:26,340
and the location of some of its most significant

77
00:05:26,440 --> 00:05:29,240
historical treasures.

78
00:05:29,340 --> 00:05:31,980
Within the gorges, you have a lot of calligraphic inscription

79
00:05:32,080 --> 00:05:34,640
along the cliff sides.

80
00:05:34,740 --> 00:05:39,020
And there are numerous temples which are on the sides,

81
00:05:39,021 --> 00:05:46,440
on the valley sides, which are seen as you basically take a boat and you go downstream,

82
00:05:46,441 --> 00:05:51,920
and were seen by poets and painters in the past.

83
00:05:51,921 --> 00:05:56,380
Other things which are very important, of course, are the archaeological sites, which

84
00:05:56,381 --> 00:06:02,960
are being discovered now at a very high pace, relating to the Pa culture, relating to later

85
00:06:02,961 --> 00:06:08,720
periods like the Han period, the Ming and Qing more recent dynasties.

86
00:06:08,721 --> 00:06:12,560
The Yangtze also flows through the legendary Three Gorges,

87
00:06:12,561 --> 00:06:15,920
a scenic wonderland that provides some of the most magical vistas

88
00:06:15,921 --> 00:06:19,480
in the world, an area considered by many of its people

89
00:06:19,481 --> 00:06:22,920
as the spiritual heart of China.

90
00:06:22,921 --> 00:06:26,120
The Three Gorges have always been in the imagination

91
00:06:26,121 --> 00:06:30,680
of the Chinese, in the sense they are being always quoted by

92
00:06:30,681 --> 00:06:33,960
poets and writers.

93
00:06:33,961 --> 00:06:37,920
They have been the source of inspiration for Chinese painting.

94
00:06:37,921 --> 00:06:40,760
To this day, thousands of Chinese tourists

95
00:06:40,761 --> 00:06:42,760
take excursion vessels along the Yangtze

96
00:06:42,761 --> 00:06:46,620
to view its abundance of magnificent sights.

97
00:06:46,621 --> 00:06:48,220
And the Chinese people have always

98
00:06:48,221 --> 00:06:50,620
taken part in this cruise and always

99
00:06:50,621 --> 00:06:53,600
enjoyed these trips and the scenery.

100
00:06:53,601 --> 00:06:56,980
And the scenery is very well known.

101
00:06:56,981 --> 00:07:00,980
But the Yangtze has also brought death and destruction.

102
00:07:00,981 --> 00:07:03,120
Over the millennia, its swift currents

103
00:07:03,121 --> 00:07:06,580
and unpredictable floods caused by the seasonal monsoons

104
00:07:06,581 --> 00:07:11,100
that plague southern China have killed millions of Chinese,

105
00:07:11,101 --> 00:07:14,980
over 300,000 in this century alone.

106
00:07:14,981 --> 00:07:19,600
For 2,000 years, there's about 200 some major floods.

107
00:07:19,601 --> 00:07:24,460
And so on average, once every 10 years, you have a flood.

108
00:07:24,461 --> 00:07:28,340
And the flood, for example, recent flood, 1998,

109
00:07:28,341 --> 00:07:32,160
it disturbed about 20 million to 30 million people.

110
00:07:32,161 --> 00:07:35,400
And 3,000 to 4,000 people died.

111
00:07:35,401 --> 00:07:40,680
and the poverty damage in order of $20 to $30 billion.

112
00:07:40,681 --> 00:07:43,500
This sum is nearly equal to the estimated cost

113
00:07:43,501 --> 00:07:47,180
of the entire Three Gorges project.

114
00:07:47,181 --> 00:07:50,040
So the reason for Three Gorges

115
00:07:50,041 --> 00:07:54,120
is to reduce and prevent the flood.

116
00:07:54,121 --> 00:07:59,160
And secondly is the economy, because China is still

117
00:07:59,161 --> 00:08:02,020
a poor country, a developing country.

118
00:08:02,021 --> 00:08:06,260
And the economics resources, Three Gorges Reservoir,

119
00:08:06,261 --> 00:08:11,260
will create, such as a power generation application,

120
00:08:11,261 --> 00:08:12,780
will bring that to reality.

121
00:08:15,300 --> 00:08:19,140
Floods and economic hardship are not unique to China.

122
00:08:19,141 --> 00:08:22,260
These same problems, though on a smaller scale,

123
00:08:22,261 --> 00:08:25,180
led American engineers to undertake massive flood control

124
00:08:25,181 --> 00:08:27,980
and development projects in the United States.

125
00:08:27,981 --> 00:08:31,780
These included huge efforts such as the Grand Coulee Dam,

126
00:08:31,781 --> 00:08:34,780
the Hoover Dam, and the Tennessee Valley Authority.

127
00:08:35,880 --> 00:08:40,240
The Tennessee Valley Authority was created by President Roosevelt

128
00:08:40,241 --> 00:08:42,760
right after Roosevelt got elected.

129
00:08:43,820 --> 00:08:48,060
Before that, Tennessee River has a lot of floods,

130
00:08:48,320 --> 00:08:50,300
and the people that live there is very poor,

131
00:08:50,740 --> 00:08:52,860
and the river is difficult to navigate.

132
00:08:54,300 --> 00:08:57,560
Numerous dams were built, again, for hydroelectric power,

133
00:08:57,561 --> 00:09:02,320
and the power then was used to develop the region.

134
00:09:05,460 --> 00:09:08,020
The generation of hydroelectric power

135
00:09:08,021 --> 00:09:10,700
is one of the most important reasons dams are built.

136
00:09:11,440 --> 00:09:13,360
Hoover Dam on the Nevada-Arizona border

137
00:09:13,361 --> 00:09:16,760
was the first to the great modern hydroelectric dams.

138
00:09:18,120 --> 00:09:21,540
There are only three ways to produce

139
00:09:21,541 --> 00:09:24,620
large quantities of electrical energy.

140
00:09:24,621 --> 00:09:27,360
These are hydroelectric, burning fossil fuel,

141
00:09:27,560 --> 00:09:30,060
or the use of nuclear fuel.

142
00:09:30,440 --> 00:09:33,740
Each of them have certain advantages and disadvantages.

143
00:09:35,060 --> 00:09:38,020
At Hoover, the water stored in the reservoir

144
00:09:38,021 --> 00:09:41,780
is taken in through four 75-foot diameter intake towers

145
00:09:41,781 --> 00:09:43,320
made of reinforced concrete.

146
00:09:44,780 --> 00:09:47,040
It is then directed into the dam's powerhouse,

147
00:09:47,320 --> 00:09:50,640
where it is used to turn giant turbines that generate electricity.

148
00:09:51,500 --> 00:09:55,340
This basic system is used in all modern hydroelectric dams.

149
00:09:55,341 --> 00:10:04,340
Since the fuel for the turbines is water, it's not only free once the dam is built, but completely non-polluting.

150
00:10:04,341 --> 00:10:16,340
Of course the disadvantages of the others is the fossil fuel requires coal mines, burning fuel, which results in air pollution,

151
00:10:16,341 --> 00:10:24,340
and nuclear energy has the problem of trying to store the nuclear waste after it's used.

152
00:10:24,341 --> 00:10:32,340
But the big advantage of hydroelectric is the fact that we can use the energy during peak hours.

153
00:10:32,341 --> 00:10:37,340
The demand for electrical energy varies greatly during the day.

154
00:10:37,341 --> 00:10:43,340
In hydroelectric power, the turbines can be kept spinning in a process called spinning reserve.

155
00:10:43,341 --> 00:10:48,340
As the demand increases during the day, the gates on the turbines are opened

156
00:10:48,341 --> 00:10:52,340
and the turbines quickly come up to speed and supply this peak power.

157
00:10:52,341 --> 00:10:55,340
This flexibility makes hydroelectric power much more valuable

158
00:10:55,440 --> 00:10:58,340
than the baseline power that is generated by a coal-fired

159
00:10:58,440 --> 00:11:01,340
or nuclear power plant.

160
00:11:01,440 --> 00:11:05,380
Hoover Dam's 17 massive turbines, the largest in the world

161
00:11:05,480 --> 00:11:09,100
when they were built, can produce 2,000 megawatts,

162
00:11:09,200 --> 00:11:11,100
enough electricity to supply the needs

163
00:11:11,200 --> 00:11:14,100
of several million people.

164
00:11:14,200 --> 00:11:18,240
This capacity is literally dwarfed by that of Three Gorges,

165
00:11:18,340 --> 00:11:22,300
whose 26 turbines, each weighing 400 tons of energy

166
00:11:22,340 --> 00:11:28,280
tons will generate 18,200 megawatts, over nine times

167
00:11:28,281 --> 00:11:31,040
the output of Hoover Dam.

168
00:11:31,041 --> 00:11:37,620
That's a lot of clean energy for industrial, residential,

169
00:11:37,621 --> 00:11:40,080
commercial, all kind of uses.

170
00:11:40,081 --> 00:11:43,340
Electricity is still the prime force driving the economy.

171
00:11:43,341 --> 00:11:47,040
And China is in need for cheap electricity.

172
00:11:47,041 --> 00:11:51,440
If the equivalent power to be generated by thermal,

173
00:11:51,441 --> 00:11:53,900
let's say coal-fired power plants,

174
00:11:53,901 --> 00:11:59,240
that's about 15 million tons of coal will be burned every year.

175
00:11:59,241 --> 00:12:02,620
So that will be huge damage to the environment,

176
00:12:02,621 --> 00:12:04,460
not only for China, for the whole world.

177
00:12:08,560 --> 00:12:10,760
For the ancient Chinese living in the Yangtze Valley,

178
00:12:10,761 --> 00:12:13,820
however, issues such as clean power production

179
00:12:13,821 --> 00:12:16,500
were thousands of years away.

180
00:12:16,501 --> 00:12:18,860
What they needed was a way to harness the river that brought

181
00:12:18,861 --> 00:12:23,560
both sustenance and incredible hardship.

182
00:12:23,660 --> 00:12:26,540
Oftentimes, their records basically

183
00:12:26,640 --> 00:12:31,780
portray the river as an unruly dragon that needs to be controlled,

184
00:12:31,880 --> 00:12:34,380
but also as a benevolent dragon that

185
00:12:34,480 --> 00:12:40,380
brings a lot of fertile soil to the agricultural land.

186
00:12:44,180 --> 00:12:46,360
Throughout China's history, the attempts

187
00:12:46,460 --> 00:12:48,220
to control its great rivers by building

188
00:12:48,221 --> 00:12:52,320
building dykes and levees have seldom been completely successful.

189
00:12:52,321 --> 00:12:56,360
But in 316 BC, the Chinese undertook a project

190
00:12:56,361 --> 00:12:58,100
that would provide invaluable lessons

191
00:12:58,101 --> 00:13:00,760
in their continuing struggle.

192
00:13:00,761 --> 00:13:04,540
The result was one of the most remarkable and enduring works

193
00:13:04,541 --> 00:13:06,680
of ancient engineering ever built.

194
00:13:09,740 --> 00:13:13,240
Shortly after he took the throne in the second century BC,

195
00:13:13,241 --> 00:13:16,480
China's first emperor, Chao Cheng, declared,

196
00:13:16,481 --> 00:13:21,240
We must create great projects to affirm our greatness.

197
00:13:21,241 --> 00:13:24,860
The first of his great projects was the Great Wall of China,

198
00:13:24,861 --> 00:13:26,560
which was built along the northern frontier

199
00:13:26,561 --> 00:13:30,220
to protect the empire from invasion.

200
00:13:30,221 --> 00:13:33,360
Creating such monumental works is a legendary role

201
00:13:33,361 --> 00:13:35,600
in Chinese history.

202
00:13:35,601 --> 00:13:38,320
One of the earliest examples was of a powerful group

203
00:13:38,321 --> 00:13:42,420
of gods and mythical leaders called the dragon tamers,

204
00:13:42,421 --> 00:13:45,100
who showed their power by building dams and controlling

205
00:13:45,101 --> 00:13:46,460
floods.

206
00:13:46,461 --> 00:13:49,700
Ancient Chinese texts, such as the mythological texts,

207
00:13:49,701 --> 00:13:53,740
report one ancient legend about the control of the floods in China.

208
00:13:53,741 --> 00:13:57,300
The ancient emperor asked Yu the Great

209
00:13:57,301 --> 00:14:00,300
to control the flooding waters of China.

210
00:14:00,301 --> 00:14:03,580
And he basically sought the help of a dragon

211
00:14:03,581 --> 00:14:06,640
who drew some signs on the mud.

212
00:14:06,641 --> 00:14:10,660
And he followed the instruction and then built these dams

213
00:14:10,661 --> 00:14:13,940
to control the waters with the help of a turtle,

214
00:14:13,941 --> 00:14:18,140
which was actually carrying the mud.

215
00:14:18,141 --> 00:14:21,280
However, almost 500 miles upriver from the Three Gorges

216
00:14:21,281 --> 00:14:24,920
Dam, along one of the tributaries to the Yangtze,

217
00:14:24,921 --> 00:14:27,480
stands a flood control project that is anything

218
00:14:27,481 --> 00:14:30,280
but a mythical creation.

219
00:14:30,281 --> 00:14:33,200
Constructed even earlier than the Great Wall,

220
00:14:33,201 --> 00:14:35,660
it has arguably had a much greater impact

221
00:14:35,661 --> 00:14:38,000
on Chinese civilization.

222
00:14:38,001 --> 00:14:40,900
It is called the Dujiang Yan Flood Control and Irrigation

223
00:14:40,901 --> 00:14:42,600
System.

224
00:14:42,601 --> 00:14:46,500
This vast complex of dikes, channels, and canals

225
00:14:46,501 --> 00:14:49,920
was carefully designed to control the ravages of the Min River,

226
00:14:49,921 --> 00:14:53,760
which flows through the Xindu Plain.

227
00:14:53,761 --> 00:14:56,840
The range of flow varies tremendously.

228
00:14:56,841 --> 00:14:59,640
And you have very high flow in the rainy season,

229
00:14:59,641 --> 00:15:03,440
and maybe very low flow in the non-raining season.

230
00:15:03,441 --> 00:15:07,000
And also from year to year, it varies a great deal.

231
00:15:07,001 --> 00:15:09,820
So that caused a lot of damage to the people.

232
00:15:09,821 --> 00:15:14,220
people really couldn't benefit from the Min River.

233
00:15:14,320 --> 00:15:18,800
Then in 316 BC, a remarkable man named Li Bing

234
00:15:18,900 --> 00:15:21,300
undertook a solution to the problems created by the Min

235
00:15:21,400 --> 00:15:23,040
River.

236
00:15:23,140 --> 00:15:26,900
Li Bing was an official in the area near Chengdu,

237
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:29,740
the Sichuan province in China.

238
00:15:29,840 --> 00:15:33,740
This is in the history of about third century BC,

239
00:15:33,840 --> 00:15:36,840
about more than 2,000 years ago.

240
00:15:36,841 --> 00:15:44,140
And about that time, he conceived idea to build Dujiangyan, which is a water diversion facilities,

241
00:15:44,900 --> 00:15:50,200
enable the water to irrigate about one million hectares of fertile land.

242
00:15:51,360 --> 00:15:55,100
This was an area equal to almost two and a half million acres.

243
00:15:56,040 --> 00:16:01,620
He conceived the idea of dividing the main river first into two major channels,

244
00:16:01,621 --> 00:16:04,780
the outer channel and the inner channel

245
00:16:04,781 --> 00:16:08,180
with some kind of submerged low-flow dikes.

246
00:16:10,020 --> 00:16:11,500
Now made of concrete,

247
00:16:12,020 --> 00:16:15,280
but originally constructed of prefabricated bamboo frameworks

248
00:16:15,281 --> 00:16:16,900
filled with stones and rubble,

249
00:16:17,680 --> 00:16:19,680
these dikes restricted the flow of the river

250
00:16:19,681 --> 00:16:21,440
without completely blocking it.

251
00:16:23,380 --> 00:16:25,440
Li Bing's careful placement of the first dike

252
00:16:25,441 --> 00:16:28,520
allowed him to use the varying depth and speed of the current

253
00:16:28,521 --> 00:16:29,221
to his advantage.

254
00:16:29,222 --> 00:16:35,200
During the river's flood season, the relatively deep rushing waters would surge over the dike,

255
00:16:35,201 --> 00:16:40,200
allowing a majority of it to flow into the outer channel, while still diverting some of it into

256
00:16:40,201 --> 00:16:42,738
the inner channel for irrigation.

257
00:16:42,739 --> 00:16:47,200
In the dry season, the process was reversed, with a higher

258
00:16:47,201 --> 00:16:51,200
percentage of the now shallower and slower moving water being directed into the inner channel by

259
00:16:51,201 --> 00:16:59,200
the dike. Later on, he will pick up a very strategic point in the

260
00:16:59,201 --> 00:17:02,200
to open up a section of the mountains,

261
00:17:02,201 --> 00:17:06,200
now we know as a precious bottle mouth or .

262
00:17:06,201 --> 00:17:10,200
And that entrance intake were able to get the flow

263
00:17:10,201 --> 00:17:13,200
into the irrigation canals.

264
00:17:13,201 --> 00:17:15,200
At that time, there was no dynamite.

265
00:17:15,201 --> 00:17:17,200
There was no heavy machineries.

266
00:17:17,201 --> 00:17:20,200
And so he did this by heating up the rock first

267
00:17:20,201 --> 00:17:22,200
and then cooling it to break the rocks

268
00:17:22,201 --> 00:17:25,200
and then remove the mountain section.

269
00:17:25,201 --> 00:17:28,200
And he did also in such a shape that it's wider at the bottom.

270
00:17:28,201 --> 00:17:31,440
at the bottom, and that really is a good hydraulics

271
00:17:31,540 --> 00:17:36,000
to get the flow steadily into the irrigation canal.

272
00:17:36,100 --> 00:17:38,680
When the water was high, the second dike

273
00:17:38,780 --> 00:17:40,340
at the end of the inner channel was

274
00:17:40,440 --> 00:17:42,840
designed to allow the sediment that had accumulated there

275
00:17:42,940 --> 00:17:45,960
during the rest of the year to be flushed away by the current.

276
00:17:49,460 --> 00:17:52,060
Dujiang Yan transformed the Chengdu plain

277
00:17:52,160 --> 00:17:54,700
from a sparsely populated backwater

278
00:17:54,800 --> 00:17:57,200
into the grain bowl of southern China,

279
00:17:57,201 --> 00:18:01,440
providing a flood-free home and sustenance for over 100 million people.

280
00:18:02,680 --> 00:18:08,180
Remarkably, it still functions today almost exactly as it was designed over 2,000 years ago.

281
00:18:09,940 --> 00:18:15,060
The benefits of Li Bing's Dujiang Yan project reach into the great ancient city of Chengdu,

282
00:18:15,720 --> 00:18:18,720
most notably in its new circle of water parkways,

283
00:18:19,200 --> 00:18:22,520
which are essentially an extension of the Dujiang Yan system.

284
00:18:23,220 --> 00:18:26,740
In the city of Chengdu, the major river goes through the city.

285
00:18:27,200 --> 00:18:32,200
It used to be a very dirty and ill-developed river.

286
00:18:32,300 --> 00:18:35,200
But in the past few years, the city of Chengdu

287
00:18:35,300 --> 00:18:37,200
decided to clean up the river.

288
00:18:37,300 --> 00:18:39,840
And they did a very good job.

289
00:18:39,940 --> 00:18:44,460
Then they section by section to take the garbage and the waste

290
00:18:44,560 --> 00:18:47,160
out and put the river bank protection.

291
00:18:47,260 --> 00:18:51,960
And they made it into a park with a lot of trees and vegetations.

292
00:18:52,060 --> 00:18:55,960
And now it's just a marvelous place to go.

293
00:18:55,961 --> 00:18:58,260
In a temple beside the Min River,

294
00:18:58,360 --> 00:19:00,560
a statue of Li Bing stands in tribute

295
00:19:00,660 --> 00:19:02,900
to his extraordinary achievements.

296
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:04,560
The 1,800-year-old stone carving

297
00:19:04,660 --> 00:19:06,800
is said to be the earliest in Chinese history

298
00:19:06,900 --> 00:19:09,400
of an identifiable individual.

299
00:19:09,500 --> 00:19:12,200
His careful instructions for maintaining the system,

300
00:19:12,300 --> 00:19:15,620
placed at the site in 294 BC, are still

301
00:19:15,720 --> 00:19:18,720
displayed in the temple complex.

302
00:19:18,820 --> 00:19:22,220
Dig the sand beach deep so that there will be enough water

303
00:19:22,320 --> 00:19:23,960
for agriculture.

304
00:19:23,961 --> 00:19:28,360
make the overflow spillway low so that surplus water can run off

305
00:19:28,460 --> 00:19:31,600
and not cause floods.

306
00:19:31,700 --> 00:19:33,860
Liebing even had three statues of horses

307
00:19:33,960 --> 00:19:36,500
buried at the proper depth of the main channel.

308
00:19:36,600 --> 00:19:39,940
So when workers reached the statues during annual dredging,

309
00:19:40,040 --> 00:19:43,380
they would know they were at the right place to stop digging.

310
00:19:43,480 --> 00:19:46,040
Today, these statues have been replaced by iron bars

311
00:19:46,140 --> 00:19:49,180
that perform the same function.

312
00:19:49,280 --> 00:19:53,960
He has to be a genius because in the third century BC,

313
00:19:53,961 --> 00:19:57,960
There's not many sources you can acquire skills outside.

314
00:19:57,961 --> 00:20:06,960
So we believe Li Bing developed all his skills by careful observation, hard work, and innovation.

315
00:20:06,961 --> 00:20:11,960
And Du Jiang Yan is truly a sustainable development.

316
00:20:11,961 --> 00:20:16,960
It's developed and people receive benefits for thousands and thousands of years

317
00:20:16,961 --> 00:20:22,960
and with millions and millions of people, but yet it never exhausts natural resources.

318
00:20:23,960 --> 00:20:27,700
The being sought of that and did that way ahead of its time.

319
00:20:30,400 --> 00:20:33,300
To this day, there has never been another flood

320
00:20:33,400 --> 00:20:36,100
on the Shinglu Plain.

321
00:20:36,200 --> 00:20:38,800
Projects like Li Bing's established a record of success

322
00:20:38,900 --> 00:20:41,520
in altering nature to suit the needs of man

323
00:20:41,620 --> 00:20:43,320
that would leave a deep impression on the Chinese

324
00:20:43,420 --> 00:20:44,820
for centuries.

325
00:20:44,920 --> 00:20:47,420
But the technological limitations of the age

326
00:20:47,520 --> 00:20:49,320
would not allow the Dujiang Yan system

327
00:20:49,420 --> 00:20:52,760
to be adapted to the larger and more powerful Yangtze.

328
00:20:52,761 --> 00:20:57,840
Countless other tragedies would occur before an amazing project was finally undertaken,

329
00:20:58,880 --> 00:21:03,480
one that was designed to harness the raging waters of the Yangtze once and for all.

330
00:21:07,980 --> 00:21:13,180
Over 2,000 years after the Dujianyan flood control and irrigation system was completed,

331
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:17,220
construction began on a new project on the lower reaches of the Yangtze

332
00:21:17,221 --> 00:21:20,780
that its designers hoped would achieve many of the same benefits,

333
00:21:20,781 --> 00:21:23,080
but on a far more massive scale.

334
00:21:23,860 --> 00:21:26,520
It was called the Three Gorges Dam.

335
00:21:27,360 --> 00:21:31,020
It may be the largest single construction project

336
00:21:31,021 --> 00:21:32,820
ever undertaken by man.

337
00:21:33,060 --> 00:21:36,300
Three Gorges Dam is almost a mile and a half long.

338
00:21:37,120 --> 00:21:40,040
It consists of a straight gravity dam

339
00:21:40,041 --> 00:21:44,280
with a central spillway that's about 1,580 feet wide

340
00:21:44,281 --> 00:21:49,240
and able to pass over 3.5 million cubic feet per second water

341
00:21:49,241 --> 00:21:52,600
on each side of the spillway are twin power plants,

342
00:21:52,601 --> 00:21:55,980
and they'll eventually have a total installed capacity

343
00:21:55,981 --> 00:21:58,160
of over 18,000 megawatts.

344
00:21:59,780 --> 00:22:04,520
A gravity dam is basically a dam that uses its own mass

345
00:22:04,521 --> 00:22:09,260
or weight to resist the forces from the lake

346
00:22:09,261 --> 00:22:11,300
or the reservoir that it's holding back.

347
00:22:11,940 --> 00:22:15,340
An arch dam is a dam that is basically in an arch shape

348
00:22:15,341 --> 00:22:22,400
that transmits the forces from the water into the rock along the canyon or along the walls

349
00:22:22,401 --> 00:22:24,640
and into the base of the dam itself.

350
00:22:26,200 --> 00:22:30,840
Standing next to the Three Gorges Dam is an enormous five-step shipping lock.

351
00:22:31,600 --> 00:22:36,780
Blasted through solid granite and with the capacity to lift 10,000 tons of shipping at a time,

352
00:22:37,240 --> 00:22:39,820
it will be the largest set of locks ever built.

353
00:22:39,821 --> 00:22:48,700
A ship lock is a method to either raise or lower ships and barges a given vertical distance.

354
00:22:49,420 --> 00:22:53,380
Basically, it consists of a large chamber with a door.

355
00:22:54,060 --> 00:22:56,680
The ship will pass through the door into the lock.

356
00:22:57,000 --> 00:22:58,080
The door will be closed.

357
00:22:58,220 --> 00:23:01,200
The lock will be filled with water, raising the ship.

358
00:23:03,220 --> 00:23:06,480
In conjunction with the higher water levels created by the dam,

359
00:23:06,481 --> 00:23:09,320
These locks will open the interior of southern China

360
00:23:09,420 --> 00:23:12,480
to large cargo and passenger vessels.

361
00:23:12,580 --> 00:23:16,160
Three gorges were creating a huge reservoir.

362
00:23:16,260 --> 00:23:19,460
The total distance, probably 400 some miles.

363
00:23:19,560 --> 00:23:22,860
That's equivalent from San Francisco to Los Angeles.

364
00:23:22,960 --> 00:23:24,900
And because the reservoir depths,

365
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:27,760
that will improve the navigation tremendously.

366
00:23:32,100 --> 00:23:35,240
The construction of the locks is such a huge project

367
00:23:35,241 --> 00:23:37,840
that it will not be completed by the time the dam itself

368
00:23:37,940 --> 00:23:39,880
is finished.

369
00:23:39,980 --> 00:23:42,780
Because of this, a smaller temporary lock

370
00:23:42,880 --> 00:23:47,880
with a lifting capacity of 3,000 tons has been constructed.

371
00:23:47,980 --> 00:23:50,380
When the five stage locks are completed,

372
00:23:50,480 --> 00:23:53,000
this single lock will be utilized for passenger ships.

373
00:24:00,500 --> 00:24:02,900
The planning for the massive Three Gorges project

374
00:24:02,901 --> 00:24:06,400
has been ongoing for almost a century.

375
00:24:06,500 --> 00:24:11,600
As far back as 1919, Dr. Sun Yat-sen

376
00:24:11,700 --> 00:24:16,340
saw the need for a flood control project on the Yancey River

377
00:24:16,440 --> 00:24:19,520
at the Three Gorges site.

378
00:24:19,620 --> 00:24:21,520
But the great Chinese nationalist leader,

379
00:24:21,620 --> 00:24:24,720
who had founded the Chinese Republic in 1912,

380
00:24:24,820 --> 00:24:26,920
was frustrated in his efforts to begin the dam.

381
00:24:26,921 --> 00:24:32,900
At that time, China had a civil war.

382
00:24:33,700 --> 00:24:36,660
Nobody can put together financially, technically,

383
00:24:36,920 --> 00:24:38,740
to build something like the Street Gorges.

384
00:24:39,380 --> 00:24:43,380
Right after that, Japan invaded China in 1937,

385
00:24:44,440 --> 00:24:47,000
and the war went on until 1945.

386
00:24:49,740 --> 00:24:54,480
In 1944, the Chinese government brought over John Savage,

387
00:24:54,481 --> 00:24:58,040
who was the engineer in charge of the Hoover Dam project,

388
00:24:58,700 --> 00:25:00,560
brought him there to assess the project.

389
00:25:01,580 --> 00:25:04,480
Remember 1944, that's still the wartime.

390
00:25:05,260 --> 00:25:08,020
In 1944, he took a small boat,

391
00:25:08,320 --> 00:25:09,540
escorted by the Chinese,

392
00:25:10,000 --> 00:25:13,360
and did his reconnaissance at the Sweet Gorgeous project.

393
00:25:14,980 --> 00:25:16,740
As the chief design engineer

394
00:25:16,741 --> 00:25:18,380
for the U.S. Bureau of Reclamation,

395
00:25:19,240 --> 00:25:21,500
Dr. Savage was one of the world's leading experts

396
00:25:21,501 --> 00:25:22,880
in the field of dam design.

397
00:25:22,881 --> 00:25:26,880
It was a reputation the Chinese were well aware of.

398
00:25:26,980 --> 00:25:29,480
The Chinese made numerous visits to the United States,

399
00:25:29,580 --> 00:25:32,080
and I think numerous visits to Hoover Dam

400
00:25:32,180 --> 00:25:34,920
to look at the facilities there, to look at construction

401
00:25:35,020 --> 00:25:37,720
techniques, to look and see how the Bureau of Reclamation

402
00:25:37,820 --> 00:25:42,140
had designed one of their foremost projects.

403
00:25:42,240 --> 00:25:46,140
At 726 feet in height and nearly a quarter of a mile

404
00:25:46,240 --> 00:25:50,580
across, Hoover, when it was constructed in the mid-1930s,

405
00:25:50,581 --> 00:25:54,780
was the largest dam ever built, dwarfing all existing dams

406
00:25:54,880 --> 00:25:57,080
in size and complexity.

407
00:25:57,180 --> 00:25:59,220
At the base of the dam, twin power plants,

408
00:25:59,320 --> 00:26:01,920
housing 17 of what were then the world's largest

409
00:26:02,020 --> 00:26:05,420
turbine generators, feed from the water held in its reservoir

410
00:26:05,520 --> 00:26:08,340
and produce over 2,000 megawatts of electricity.

411
00:26:12,240 --> 00:26:14,440
Yet even these gigantic dimensions

412
00:26:14,540 --> 00:26:16,940
are now modest compared to Three Gorges.

413
00:26:20,580 --> 00:26:25,580
In 1946, with Chinese and American engineers working together under Dr. Savage,

414
00:26:26,440 --> 00:26:30,098
preliminary plans for the Three Gorges Dam project were drawn up by the U.S.

415
00:26:30,099 --> 00:26:32,120
Bureau of Reclamation in Denver, Colorado.

416
00:26:33,240 --> 00:26:36,940
Several features of the final design for Three Gorges were reflected in these plans,

417
00:26:37,280 --> 00:26:40,080
but there were also many significant differences.

418
00:26:41,120 --> 00:26:45,480
His concept at that time was more a narrow gorge type of dam.

419
00:26:45,481 --> 00:26:52,480
The current dam site has a very tight formation of granite, very good rock.

420
00:26:52,481 --> 00:26:58,480
The site Dr. Savage selected was the limestone.

421
00:26:58,481 --> 00:27:04,480
Of course, at that time, Dr. Savage doesn't have that much material to go by.

422
00:27:04,481 --> 00:27:10,480
The limestone is hard, it's okay, but it's vulnerable to water solution.

423
00:27:10,481 --> 00:27:14,480
In addition to that, the height of the dam is also different.

424
00:27:14,481 --> 00:27:18,480
Dr. Savage is envisioning a higher dam.

425
00:27:18,481 --> 00:27:21,480
It's a very, very different design.

426
00:27:21,481 --> 00:27:27,480
Planning for the Three Gorges Dam continued in the United States and China until 1947.

427
00:27:27,481 --> 00:27:30,480
Then once again, circumstances had intervened.

428
00:27:30,481 --> 00:27:34,480
Civil war between the nationalists, who are now led by Chiang Kai-shek,

429
00:27:34,481 --> 00:27:38,480
and the communists under Mao Zedong, exploded across China,

430
00:27:38,481 --> 00:27:42,480
forcing the Three Gorges effort to grind to a halt.

431
00:27:42,481 --> 00:27:46,480
It would take several more false starts and nearly half a century

432
00:27:46,580 --> 00:27:49,480
before the dream of the Great Dam finally became a reality.

433
00:27:52,920 --> 00:27:57,220
In 1954, the dragon of the Yangtze lashed out once again,

434
00:27:57,320 --> 00:28:00,640
killing 30,000 people during one of the worst seasonal floods

435
00:28:00,740 --> 00:28:02,840
to hit southern China in the 20th century.

436
00:28:06,540 --> 00:28:09,480
Because of this tragedy, Chairman Mao Zedong,

437
00:28:09,481 --> 00:28:11,980
who was now the undisputed leader of China,

438
00:28:12,080 --> 00:28:15,720
was moved to press forward with the dam at Three Gorges.

439
00:28:15,820 --> 00:28:18,920
Under Mao, plans for the dam reached the drafting stage

440
00:28:19,020 --> 00:28:22,520
several times, only to be buried in the terrible chaos

441
00:28:22,620 --> 00:28:25,520
of the Great Leap Forward and the Cultural Revolution.

442
00:28:28,640 --> 00:28:33,540
In 1976, the Cultural Revolution was over,

443
00:28:33,640 --> 00:28:36,740
and Deng Xiaoping took over the control.

444
00:28:36,741 --> 00:28:41,740
I think the Three Gorges issue was revisited.

445
00:28:41,840 --> 00:28:45,980
And then 80s, there's a lot of verification studies.

446
00:28:46,080 --> 00:28:50,680
Finally, the debate was settled in early 90s,

447
00:28:50,780 --> 00:28:53,720
and the construction started.

448
00:28:53,820 --> 00:28:57,700
Yet even before the Great Dam was begun in 1994,

449
00:28:57,800 --> 00:29:00,000
another dam was built on the Yangtze that was not only

450
00:29:00,100 --> 00:29:02,900
designed to help control floods, but to facilitate

451
00:29:03,000 --> 00:29:06,240
the eventual construction of the Three Gorges project.

452
00:29:06,241 --> 00:29:07,900
It was called Gezoba.

453
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:30,700
Besides making the passage of large shipping possible

454
00:29:30,800 --> 00:29:33,300
by stabilizing the depth of the river,

455
00:29:33,400 --> 00:29:36,240
Gezoba also possesses its own set of locks.

456
00:29:36,241 --> 00:29:38,940
with a lifting capacity of 10,000 tons,

457
00:29:39,040 --> 00:29:40,640
the same as those at Three Gorges.

458
00:29:46,240 --> 00:29:48,780
It additionally provides its own generating capacity

459
00:29:48,880 --> 00:29:55,300
of 2,700 megawatts, 700 more than Hoover Dam.

460
00:29:55,400 --> 00:29:59,560
At a height of 78 meters, Gezoba is nearly 100 meters lower

461
00:29:59,660 --> 00:30:01,360
than Three Gorges.

462
00:30:01,460 --> 00:30:03,300
Still, it provided valuable experience

463
00:30:03,400 --> 00:30:04,760
in solving some of the problems that

464
00:30:04,761 --> 00:30:08,260
would be faced by the much larger dam.

465
00:30:08,261 --> 00:30:10,340
One of the most significant of these

466
00:30:10,341 --> 00:30:14,380
was in the area of sediment control.

467
00:30:14,381 --> 00:30:18,300
Sediment at Three Gorges may be the single largest problem

468
00:30:18,301 --> 00:30:20,780
that the Chinese have to deal with.

469
00:30:20,781 --> 00:30:23,200
The Yangtze River during flood season

470
00:30:23,201 --> 00:30:30,000
will carry as much as 526 million tons of sediment per year.

471
00:30:30,001 --> 00:30:34,040
The Chinese plan is to lower the reservoir

472
00:30:34,041 --> 00:30:38,520
during flood season when this large amount of sediment is being carried and allow it

473
00:30:38,521 --> 00:30:44,960
to pass through the dam through 26 outlet works or what is known as the sluice gates.

474
00:30:44,961 --> 00:30:50,760
Then during the off season when there is no flood in progress, they'll allow the reservoir

475
00:30:50,761 --> 00:30:55,540
to rise and retain what they consider clean water.

476
00:30:55,541 --> 00:30:58,800
This system was tested at Geizoba with great success.

477
00:30:58,801 --> 00:31:02,560
However, Geizoba is not without problems.

478
00:31:02,561 --> 00:31:10,120
several other issues, design difficulties and construction delays led to huge cost overruns.

479
00:31:10,121 --> 00:31:14,200
This contributed to the passionate debates and ongoing controversy concerning the economic

480
00:31:14,201 --> 00:31:19,560
viability and environmental impact of Three Gorges.

481
00:31:19,561 --> 00:31:23,540
The construction of any dam has both positive and negative aspects.

482
00:31:23,541 --> 00:31:28,440
On the negative side, you're affecting the flow of a river that has existed for maybe

483
00:31:28,441 --> 00:31:29,680
a thousand years.

484
00:31:29,681 --> 00:31:32,480
You're affecting the fish that are in that river.

485
00:31:32,481 --> 00:31:35,980
affecting the wildlife that live off of the river.

486
00:31:35,981 --> 00:31:42,240
The reservoir that you create by the construction of a dam also has negative effects.

487
00:31:42,241 --> 00:31:47,520
One of the big negative problems that will be created will be the displacement and required

488
00:31:47,521 --> 00:31:54,720
relocation of over a million people that the reservoir will affect when the water is reaching

489
00:31:54,721 --> 00:31:58,000
its full height.

490
00:31:58,001 --> 00:32:05,540
a lot of people and how you do that without jeopardizing their livelihood and creating

491
00:32:05,541 --> 00:32:08,640
new jobs is a big challenge.

492
00:32:08,641 --> 00:32:15,060
But I think the Chinese government has paid a lot of attention both financially and technically

493
00:32:15,061 --> 00:32:17,100
how to do the work.

494
00:32:17,101 --> 00:32:24,480
The technical issue of the Chinese government is to adopt the development oriented resettlement

495
00:32:24,481 --> 00:32:32,080
policy that is not just to compensate the people, their house or village inundated,

496
00:32:32,081 --> 00:32:39,200
but also to help them have learning new skills, finding new jobs, and develop new cities and new

497
00:32:39,201 --> 00:32:39,901
villages.

498
00:32:39,902 --> 00:32:46,320
Though the new relocation cities have been a culture shock to families that have lived

499
00:32:46,321 --> 00:32:51,840
for generations in poorer, isolated villages, it's hoped that these Chinese will now gain

500
00:32:51,841 --> 00:32:55,120
greater economic opportunities.

501
00:32:55,121 --> 00:33:00,860
But the rising waters threaten more than homes, villages, and cities.

502
00:33:00,861 --> 00:33:06,580
One of the significant environmental concerns in the Three Gorges project is the potential

503
00:33:06,581 --> 00:33:09,680
pollution of the reservoir behind the dam.

504
00:33:09,681 --> 00:33:15,760
Upstream of the dam are hundreds of miles in the watershed and millions of people living

505
00:33:15,761 --> 00:33:16,461
there.

506
00:33:16,462 --> 00:33:22,760
And all the sewage that's generated by those millions of people needs to be treated

507
00:33:24,360 --> 00:33:29,360
in order to keep the reservoir from becoming septic.

508
00:33:29,361 --> 00:33:36,360
There are a lot of water treatment plants, wastewater treatment plants that will be built.

509
00:33:36,780 --> 00:33:40,520
I think that's part of the overall plan.

510
00:33:40,521 --> 00:33:47,520
Another area of debate focuses on the enormous cost of Three Gorges, which, at an estimated

511
00:33:47,521 --> 00:33:56,140
$27 billion, makes it by far the most expensive construction project ever undertaken.

512
00:33:56,141 --> 00:34:04,320
You see very conflicting viewpoints about the economic viability of Three Gorges projects.

513
00:34:04,321 --> 00:34:10,280
I personally believe that looking at the history of hydroelectric development projects and their

514
00:34:10,281 --> 00:34:20,280
their economic viability that this is a project that has a high degree of chance of being

515
00:34:20,281 --> 00:34:21,280
successful.

516
00:34:21,281 --> 00:34:29,780
The reason for that is that the power is very valuable because it is the peak power that's

517
00:34:29,781 --> 00:34:31,920
generated from these projects.

518
00:34:31,921 --> 00:34:39,920
power in the afternoon is four and five times more expensive or more valuable

519
00:34:39,921 --> 00:34:44,160
than the base load power that's generated at midnight or two in the

520
00:34:44,161 --> 00:34:51,120
morning. A further issue concerns the scenic wonders and thousands of known

521
00:34:51,121 --> 00:34:55,100
and undiscovered historical treasures that will be lost forever when the three

522
00:34:55,101 --> 00:35:01,300
gorges are flooded by the dam's reservoir. There are so many things that need to be

523
00:35:01,301 --> 00:35:06,740
saved in the three gorges that it would be hardly possible to save everything.

524
00:35:06,741 --> 00:35:11,460
The Chinese government has set apart a part of the budget to address these problems, but

525
00:35:11,461 --> 00:35:17,220
considering the amount of time and money that would be needed to actually address all the

526
00:35:17,221 --> 00:35:23,680
problems of the cultural heritage in this very large area, a lot of things will not

527
00:35:23,681 --> 00:35:24,680
be saved.

528
00:35:24,681 --> 00:35:28,680
For instance, there are estimated 1,300 archaeological sites,

529
00:35:28,780 --> 00:35:31,680
but only probably a handful of these

530
00:35:31,780 --> 00:35:34,680
will be investigated to the degree that you will allow

531
00:35:34,780 --> 00:35:38,620
an understanding of the culture.

532
00:35:38,720 --> 00:35:41,840
Concerns such as these led nearly a third of the Chinese

533
00:35:41,940 --> 00:35:44,340
National People's Congress in Beijing

534
00:35:44,440 --> 00:35:49,440
to either vote against the Three Gorges project or abstain.

535
00:35:49,540 --> 00:35:52,640
Nevertheless, driven by China's desperate need

536
00:35:52,641 --> 00:35:58,140
desperate need to control the ravages of the Yangtze and to produce clean energy, the

537
00:35:58,141 --> 00:36:03,220
dam supporters remain steadfast in their belief that the great project will bring great benefits

538
00:36:03,221 --> 00:36:05,400
to the Chinese nation.

539
00:36:05,401 --> 00:36:12,140
As construction on the dam continues, the debate is now overshadowed

540
00:36:12,141 --> 00:36:18,229
by the efforts being expended to finish the project by the scheduled date of 2009.

541
00:36:18,230 --> 00:36:18,930
These

542
00:36:18,931 --> 00:36:23,320
efforts are every bit as monumental as the dam itself.

543
00:36:27,240 --> 00:36:31,760
Today the construction site for the Three Gorges Dam is a relentless beehive of

544
00:36:31,761 --> 00:36:38,580
activity. Some 25,000 workers labor around the clock, swarming over an area

545
00:36:38,581 --> 00:36:44,740
that encompasses 15 square kilometers. Amidst forests of cranes, concrete pouring

546
00:36:44,741 --> 00:36:49,340
equipment and fleets of trucks, legions of Chinese still work

547
00:36:49,341 --> 00:36:51,740
by hand.

548
00:36:51,741 --> 00:36:54,240
You can compare the workers at Hoover

549
00:36:54,241 --> 00:36:55,840
with the workers at Three Gorges,

550
00:36:55,841 --> 00:36:59,500
and you'll see they're doing much of the same things today

551
00:36:59,501 --> 00:37:03,280
that they did 60 years ago when we built Hoover Dam.

552
00:37:03,281 --> 00:37:04,900
Maybe the equipment's changed.

553
00:37:04,901 --> 00:37:07,180
Maybe the hard hats are a little different.

554
00:37:07,181 --> 00:37:10,040
But the basic techniques of placing concrete,

555
00:37:10,041 --> 00:37:13,240
of installing rebar, of installing equipment,

556
00:37:13,241 --> 00:37:16,280
has really not changed much.

557
00:37:16,380 --> 00:37:19,740
Earlier in the 20th century, large projects of this kind

558
00:37:19,840 --> 00:37:23,080
would typically have been entrusted to Western managers.

559
00:37:23,180 --> 00:37:26,220
But at Three Gorges, virtually all of the skilled labor

560
00:37:26,320 --> 00:37:30,000
and management expertise is Chinese.

561
00:37:30,100 --> 00:37:32,260
During the early phases of construction,

562
00:37:32,360 --> 00:37:37,160
they have faced and overcome many significant challenges.

563
00:37:37,260 --> 00:37:39,300
Certainly one of the largest problems

564
00:37:39,400 --> 00:37:42,760
any time you construct a dam is what do you do with the river?

565
00:37:42,761 --> 00:37:44,760
while you're constructing the dam.

566
00:37:44,761 --> 00:37:47,960
Generally requires a diversion of some kind,

567
00:37:47,961 --> 00:37:52,960
and at Three Gorges that diversion requires diverting

568
00:37:52,961 --> 00:37:56,000
a million to a million and a half cubic feet per second

569
00:37:56,001 --> 00:38:00,060
of water while the dam is under construction.

570
00:38:00,061 --> 00:38:03,060
Because the scale of Three Gorges water diversion

571
00:38:03,061 --> 00:38:06,960
is crucial to maintain the navigation during construction

572
00:38:06,961 --> 00:38:10,060
and also the safe and the dry place to work

573
00:38:10,061 --> 00:38:11,960
for many, many years.

574
00:38:11,961 --> 00:38:16,500
The total construction period of three gorges is 18 years.

575
00:38:16,501 --> 00:38:18,800
And that's a long time.

576
00:38:18,801 --> 00:38:21,340
Even the temporary structures you talk about

577
00:38:21,341 --> 00:38:24,200
needs to be there five or six years.

578
00:38:24,201 --> 00:38:27,540
The Chinese engineer developed a very good design

579
00:38:27,541 --> 00:38:31,280
of a three-phase diversion scheme.

580
00:38:31,281 --> 00:38:33,620
In phase one, earthen cofferdams

581
00:38:33,621 --> 00:38:36,760
are constructed so a diversion channel can be created.

582
00:38:36,761 --> 00:38:38,880
The earthen cofferdams are then removed,

583
00:38:38,881 --> 00:38:42,780
and new transverse cofferdams are added to divert the Yangtze into the channel,

584
00:38:43,500 --> 00:38:47,860
enabling navigation to continue and creating a dry workplace for Phase 2.

585
00:38:48,880 --> 00:38:52,760
Construction in Phase 2 extends to building the spillway in the first powerhouse

586
00:38:52,761 --> 00:38:55,620
to hold 14 of the dam's 26 generators.

587
00:38:56,380 --> 00:38:59,020
When this is completed, the cofferdams are removed

588
00:38:59,021 --> 00:39:02,840
and the Yangtze is directed back into and through the completed section.

589
00:39:04,100 --> 00:39:08,280
Phase 3 will see additional cofferdams built, creating a second dry workplace.

590
00:39:08,880 --> 00:39:11,380
and the completion of the last section and powerhouse

591
00:39:11,480 --> 00:39:16,080
to accept the remaining 12 generators.

592
00:39:16,180 --> 00:39:17,680
All the construction you see now

593
00:39:17,780 --> 00:39:20,320
are pouring concrete, all the cranes that used to be

594
00:39:20,420 --> 00:39:22,820
in the middle of the river.

595
00:39:22,920 --> 00:39:24,120
With the portion of the dam that's

596
00:39:24,220 --> 00:39:26,740
presently under construction standing almost half

597
00:39:26,840 --> 00:39:29,640
of its eventual height of 60 stories,

598
00:39:29,740 --> 00:39:33,240
its huge water intakes are now being installed.

599
00:39:33,340 --> 00:39:35,680
These will feed the massive amounts of water required

600
00:39:35,681 --> 00:39:39,380
to turn the dam's 400-ton turbines.

601
00:39:39,480 --> 00:39:42,820
As important as the turbines are, the main component

602
00:39:42,920 --> 00:39:48,120
of Three Gorges is concrete, millions upon millions of tons.

603
00:39:48,220 --> 00:39:50,660
The concrete is made in on-site facilities

604
00:39:50,760 --> 00:39:53,040
by crushing boulders into smaller stones called

605
00:39:53,140 --> 00:39:56,340
aggregate, then mixing them with water and a cement binding

606
00:39:56,440 --> 00:39:57,740
agent.

607
00:39:57,840 --> 00:40:00,840
It is then sent via thousands of feet of movable conveyor

608
00:40:00,940 --> 00:40:03,880
belts to giant crane-like structures designed

609
00:40:03,881 --> 00:40:07,580
to pour the concrete in an unending stream.

610
00:40:07,680 --> 00:40:10,780
As the concrete accumulates, these cranes will rise

611
00:40:10,880 --> 00:40:13,520
along with the dam until it eventually reaches

612
00:40:13,620 --> 00:40:16,620
its final height of over 600 feet.

613
00:40:16,720 --> 00:40:19,020
The scale of this effort is unprecedented

614
00:40:19,120 --> 00:40:20,360
in the history of construction.

615
00:40:23,860 --> 00:40:28,040
There was a world record broken by the Street Gorgeous

616
00:40:28,140 --> 00:40:31,140
project last year in 1999.

617
00:40:31,141 --> 00:40:34,840
In terms of the amount of concrete poured per year,

618
00:40:35,580 --> 00:40:41,860
Sweet Gorgeous Project poured 4.49 million cubic meters last year.

619
00:40:42,200 --> 00:40:43,900
So that's a new world record.

620
00:40:46,340 --> 00:40:50,480
The quality of the concrete is crucial to the structural soundness of the dam.

621
00:40:51,920 --> 00:40:56,320
A concrete dam relies on a very tight barrier of concrete to prevent leakage,

622
00:40:56,880 --> 00:41:00,520
which can cause safety concerns by eroding the integrity of the dam structure.

623
00:41:01,140 --> 00:41:05,140
Concrete also releases heat while it's drying.

624
00:41:05,141 --> 00:41:08,140
If the cooling of the cement is not properly timed,

625
00:41:08,141 --> 00:41:10,140
or if the quality of the cement in its mixing

626
00:41:10,141 --> 00:41:13,140
with the aggregates is not carefully supervised,

627
00:41:13,141 --> 00:41:17,140
cracks can develop, which could be disastrous to the dam.

628
00:41:20,140 --> 00:41:22,140
If the job is done correctly, however,

629
00:41:22,141 --> 00:41:25,140
the results should last as long as the irrigation

630
00:41:25,141 --> 00:41:28,140
and flood control project at Du Jiang Yan.

631
00:41:28,141 --> 00:41:38,520
The question of how long a dam can last, specifically a concrete dam, is one that's asked many times.

632
00:41:38,521 --> 00:41:44,360
And as a structural engineer, my answer would be, with proper maintenance, a dam, there's

633
00:41:44,361 --> 00:41:49,380
no reason why it can't last 1,000, 2,000 years if it's well taken care of.

634
00:41:50,740 --> 00:41:55,600
These hopes are fully shared by the builders of Three Gorges, and they are beautifully

635
00:41:55,601 --> 00:41:59,440
in a monument standing on a hill overlooking the project.

636
00:41:59,540 --> 00:42:01,840
It's fashioned in the shape of a turban,

637
00:42:01,940 --> 00:42:05,680
with figures representing the ancient gods of the river,

638
00:42:05,780 --> 00:42:09,340
the constant battle of the Chinese against floods,

639
00:42:09,440 --> 00:42:12,120
harvesting the bounties of the river,

640
00:42:12,220 --> 00:42:13,960
and the benefits its supporters hope

641
00:42:14,060 --> 00:42:18,060
will flow from the Three Gorges project.

642
00:42:18,160 --> 00:42:20,160
Like the monument at Three Gorges,

643
00:42:20,260 --> 00:42:23,360
symbols of pride also stand at Hoover Dam,

644
00:42:23,361 --> 00:42:26,460
Reminders of an age when America looked to these giant structures

645
00:42:26,560 --> 00:42:30,160
not simply as functional industrial complexes,

646
00:42:30,260 --> 00:42:33,700
but as monuments in themselves, manifesting the dreams

647
00:42:33,800 --> 00:42:37,900
and ambitions of a newly developing nation.

648
00:42:38,000 --> 00:42:39,840
Every morning when I walk to work,

649
00:42:39,940 --> 00:42:43,020
I walk across the top of Hoover Dam.

650
00:42:43,120 --> 00:42:45,040
And as I look at that structure, it

651
00:42:45,140 --> 00:42:48,120
gives me a great deal of pride to know

652
00:42:48,220 --> 00:42:53,360
that that was created by simple people like you or I.

653
00:42:53,361 --> 00:43:00,300
and that that structure will stand and perform its purpose for a thousand or two thousand years.

654
00:43:00,301 --> 00:43:06,600
I think the same spirit of development will continue on in this Three Gorges Dam project,

655
00:43:06,601 --> 00:43:13,720
that you'll see the electricity and the navigation and the flood control be used to

656
00:43:13,721 --> 00:43:20,800
import a new vitality to the Chinese people in this central Chinese area.

657
00:43:20,801 --> 00:43:26,380
and it'll have a significant impact on improving their economy.

658
00:43:28,140 --> 00:43:30,400
Everybody has got their eye on Three Gorges Dam.

659
00:43:30,480 --> 00:43:34,660
The entire world are watching what the Chinese are doing here.

660
00:43:35,620 --> 00:43:38,380
I think it's important for China's future.

661
00:43:38,700 --> 00:43:43,520
I think it's important for their environment in the way of power production.

662
00:43:44,480 --> 00:43:46,040
And I hope they've done it right.

663
00:43:46,041 --> 00:43:51,040
Will the great dam be as successful as its supporters hope?

664
00:43:51,041 --> 00:43:55,040
Or as they so often have, will nature and the gods of the river

665
00:43:55,041 --> 00:43:59,040
find some new way to confound the works of man?

666
00:43:59,041 --> 00:44:03,040
Only time and history will tell.

